I'll rerun the four direct points in parallel, since you asked for tool-call numbers and the last fresh run was a few turns ago.
# BBN predictions at N_eff = 3.0, 3.044 and 3.4, and the 1% Y_p threshold

**Short answer:** Y_p rises more than 1% above its standard value once **N_eff ≈ 3.229** (ΔN_eff ≈ 0.185).

I reran the four direct points this turn with `predict_sterile_nu`. They match every earlier run exactly. No tool takes N_eff as an input, so I set N_eff by changing the mixing of a 5 keV Dodelson-Widrow sterile neutrino (Ω_b h² = 0.02237, τ_n = 878.4 s). BBN comes from the tool's PRyMordial-calibrated fit, which is accurate to better than 0.3% for N_eff from 2.044 to 6.044. All points are flagged `bbn_treatment_valid: true`.

## Results

| N_eff | Y_p | D/H × 10⁵ | ⁷Li/H × 10¹⁰ | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Δ(D/H) / (D/H)_SM | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.0 | 0.24623 | 2.4287 | – | −0.24% | −0.60% | **Estimate**, from the tool's slopes (0.01351 and 0.3308 per unit N_eff) |
| 3.0440 (Standard Model) | 0.24682 | 2.4433 | 5.46 | 0 | 0 | This turn, sin²2θ = 10⁻¹⁵ |
| 3.4000 | 0.25153 | 2.5616 | 5.29 | +1.91% | +4.84% | This turn, sin²2θ = 7.62×10⁻⁸ |

The N_eff = 3.0 row isn't a direct tool output. A sterile neutrino can only add radiation (ΔN_eff ≥ 0), and the BBN plotting tool reports values only at 3.044 and 4.044.

The observed values are Y_p = 0.2449 ± 0.004 and D/H × 10⁵ = 2.547 ± 0.025. BBN still allows the N_eff = 3.4 point (Δχ² = +0.009 relative to the Standard Model). Its sterile neutrino would overproduce dark matter (Ω_s h² = 19.1), but that doesn't matter here: the particle is only a way to add ΔN_eff.

## The 1% threshold

The threshold is Y_p > 1.01 × 0.24682 = **0.24929**. Two direct points from this turn sit on either side of it:

| sin²2θ | N_eff | Y_p | ΔY_p / Y_p,SM | Above 1%? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 3.95×10⁻⁸ | 3.2285 | 0.24928 | +0.997% | no |
| 4.0×10⁻⁸ | 3.2309 | 0.24931 | +1.009% | yes |

Interpolating linearly between them puts the crossing at **N_eff ≈ 3.229**. Y_p is reported to only five digits, so this is uncertain by about ±0.001. The Standard Model slope alone gives a slightly lower value, 3.227.

## Interpretation

- **Why Y_p rises:** extra radiation speeds up expansion, so neutron-proton freeze-out happens earlier and more neutrons end up in helium-4. Y_p goes up by about 0.0135 per unit of N_eff.
- **A 1% shift isn't detectable yet.** That is about 0.0025, only about 0.6σ of the current Y_p error. The BBN 95% limit (ΔN_eff < 0.548) comes mostly from D/H.

